WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.500
For all the billions of dollars and&nbsp;
years of work invested in making a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.240
things sometimes go terribly wrong. Here&nbsp;
are some films that are out there somewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.640
on a shelf, but have never been released.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.880
With a cast including Bill Murray, Dan Aykroyd,&nbsp;
and Zach "Gremlins" Galligan, 1984's Nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.320
Lasts Forever is the strange brainchild&nbsp;
of Saturday Night Live alum Tom Schiller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.360
who wrote and directed the film, and the show's&nbsp;
producer, Lorne Michaels. Due to unknown legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.620
issues, the film never made it to either the&nbsp;
big screen or home release, though it's played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.516
on German and Dutch TV and was granted a single&nbsp;
unceremonious airing on Turner Classic Movies.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.900
"You will get everything&nbsp;
you want in your lifetime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.164
only you won't get it in the way you expect."

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:47.040
The film takes place in a Brazil-like&nbsp;
surrealist world where the Port Authority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.880
has actual authority over all of New&nbsp;
York, underground hobos rule the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.263
and Bill Murray is a bus driver&nbsp;
who makes trips to the moon.

00:00:54.263 --> 00:00:58.740
"Try to keep away from conceptual art."

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.880
The whole thing seems too strange to&nbsp;
exist, but a trailer lives on YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.100
and, if you dig long enough, you may be able to&nbsp;
find an unauthorized copy of the whole thing.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.340
Horror fans are serious about continuity, and the&nbsp;
complicated mess of sequels, prequels, and reboots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:19.740
released after Tobe Hooper's The Texas Chain Saw&nbsp;
Massacre don't make it easy to follow. In 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.180
the director's son, William Hooper, added his own&nbsp;
entry to the canon with All American Massacre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.400
a 1970s-set prequel to The Texas Chainsaw&nbsp;
Massacre 2 that has never been publicly released.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:34.020
The film's biggest bonus is that it includes&nbsp;
actor Bill Moseley reprising the role of Chop Top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.580
Leatherface's deranged brother. It also promised&nbsp;
to delve into the origin story of the murderous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.720
Sawyer family. Years later, William turned&nbsp;
to Kickstarter to complete post-production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.240
but failed to secure the $8,000 funding — possibly&nbsp;
due to the trailer that accompanied the campaign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.700
which struck some fans as amateurish. The film's&nbsp;
Facebook page has gone from intermittent updates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.440
to silence, so it's possible the film will&nbsp;
never see the light of day. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.720
the franchise has migrated from studio to studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.448
with the latest reboot, Texas Chainsaw&nbsp;
Massacre, released by Netflix in 2022.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.140
Conceived in the tradition of terrible&nbsp;
1980s college frat movies, Spring Break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.080
'83 features a cross-section of&nbsp;
stars from today and yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:18.240
including a pre-Reacher Alan Ritchson, Erik&nbsp;
Estrada, John Goodman, Morgan Fairchild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.340
Joe Pantoliano, and even Lee Majors from The Six&nbsp;
Million Dollar Man. The plot centered around a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.280
group of four friends who get their revenge&nbsp;
after being bullied by the rest of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.380
high school graduating class. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
we'll probably never know how they do it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:35.201
whether it's funny since production shut&nbsp;
down because they weren't paying anyone.

00:02:35.201 --> 00:02:38.861
After the plug was pulled, additional&nbsp;
filming went forward a few months later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.861 --> 00:02:42.900
and director Mars Callahan told followers&nbsp;
that his cut was complete. Big Sky Pictures'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.100
now defunct website posted dozens of&nbsp;
production photos and even a preview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.204
of the beginning of the film, but&nbsp;
to date, the film is stuck in limbo.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.340
In 2012, Tom Hooper, the director of Les&nbsp;
Misérables did something that seemed new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.360
by recording the film's entire musical&nbsp;
score live, with the actors singing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.100
set and without any fancy studio overdubs,&nbsp;
even when they may have needed them. But Les&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:10.020
Misérables wasn't the first film to be produced&nbsp;
this way. Take, for example, 2004's Temptation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.734
directed by Mark Tarlov, which we'll never get&nbsp;
to see, even though it employed actual singers.

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:20.172
Temptation featured an all-star cast of&nbsp;
Broadway notables like Adam Pascal from Rent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.172 --> 00:03:25.104
as well as future Avatar, Star Trek, and&nbsp;
Guardians of the Galaxy star Zoe Saldana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:28.764
The rock opera is reportedly a musical&nbsp;
interpretation of the Faust legend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.764 --> 00:03:32.580
mixed with a murder mystery and a&nbsp;
sprinkling of surrealistic animation.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:36.480
The film was only shown once, at&nbsp;
the 2004 New York Musical Festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.508
but producers claim to want to finish the film,&nbsp;
despite director Mark Tarlov's death in 2021.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.500
Dutch director Richard Raaphorst's horror film,&nbsp;
Worst Case Scenario, is about an army of German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.660
zombies that rises from the dead to exact revenge&nbsp;
against England over a devastating soccer loss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:57.528
Allegory? Maybe. But whatever its merits, it's&nbsp;
unclear how much of the movie actually exists.

00:03:57.528 --> 00:04:01.740
Filming began in 2004, but only a little&nbsp;
footage has been seen, mostly in a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:07.320
of popular trailers. In fact, the official promo&nbsp;
was nominated for a 2006 Golden Trailer Award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:11.820
and some zombie fans dubbed it the best&nbsp;
trailer they'd ever seen. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:16.680
it's likely the trailer is all we'll ever see of&nbsp;
the movie. The production was canceled in 2009.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.180
If there's a lesson to be learned from&nbsp;
Batgirl, it might be that no property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:25.704
is safe from the whims of studios, even a&nbsp;
proven multimedia franchise like Batman.

00:04:25.704 --> 00:04:27.504
"Batman speaking."

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.580
Warner Bros. Discovery dropped $90 million&nbsp;
to bring Batgirl to movie screens. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:40.200
loaded with talent, including Leslie Grace&nbsp;
as Barbara Gordon; J.K. Simmons as her dad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.700
Commissioner James Gordon; Brendan Fraser&nbsp;
as the villain, Firefly; and Michael Keaton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:48.900
who reprised his role as Batman. With Adil&nbsp;
El Arbi and Bilall Fallah behind the camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:53.807
it seemed, at least on paper, that&nbsp;
Batgirl was headed for blockbuster status.

00:04:53.807 --> 00:04:58.860
But something went wrong. Warner Bros. Discovery&nbsp;
canceled plans to release the film to theaters or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:04.080
even broadcast it on Max. The move was shocking,&nbsp;
given that Batgirl was still in post-production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.400
at the time of the decision. No official reason&nbsp;
was given for the move, though industry experts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.500
have pointed to several allegedly negative test&nbsp;
screenings and the potential tax write-off for WBD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.480
to drop the expensive project. There are currently&nbsp;
no plans to release Batgirl to any platform.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.760
Sorry, Aaron Taylor-Johnson: you aren't the&nbsp;
first live-action incarnation of Kraven the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.740
Hunter. An unnamed actor played the formidable&nbsp;
Spider-Man foe in a 1974 fan film produced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.700
students at New York University. The short,&nbsp;
titled Spider-Man versus Kraven the Hunter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.260
was a 30-minute color film shot on 16mm&nbsp;
by Bruce Cardozo {kar-DOH-zoh}, who would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.660
later provide visual effects&nbsp;
for The Avengers and Thor.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:44.700
Cardozo's film drew on The Amazing Spider-Man&nbsp;
#15, which kicked off Kraven's long run as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:48.900
Spider-Man adversary. According to those who&nbsp;
saw it, Kraven was faithful to the tone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.100
look of Stan Lee and Steve Ditko's comics,&nbsp;
and even featured extensive visual effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.700
that allowed Joe Elliott's Spider-Man to&nbsp;
crawl up buildings. The finished product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:01.692
was so good it earned a thumbs-up from Stan Lee&nbsp;
and played at comic book conventions for decades.

00:06:01.692 --> 00:06:06.600
Kraven won Marvel's support but not its financial&nbsp;
backing or distribution rights. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:11.700
the film languished in Cardozo's personal&nbsp;
collection until his death in 2015. Reportedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:16.020
the only known copy of the film was stored&nbsp;
on Cardozo's computer, which was destroyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:19.860
around the time of his death. Currently,&nbsp;
it exists only as a handful of screenshots.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:25.500
After several hits with low-budget martial arts&nbsp;
films like Bloodsport 2, siblings and producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:30.720
partners Alan and Diane Mehrez set their sights&nbsp;
on the mainstream market. They sunk $5 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.160
into a gangster-themed comedy-drama called The&nbsp;
Good Life and hired a cast of familiar faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:40.320
including Dennis Hopper, Andrew Dice Clay,&nbsp;
mob movie vets Frank Vincent and Tony Sirico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.364
and Frank Stallone, who also corralled&nbsp;
his brother, Sylvester, into a cameo.

00:06:44.364 --> 00:06:49.200
But the film was in trouble from the start,&nbsp;
and after only 15 days, unions picketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.040
the largely non-union crew shut the film&nbsp;
down. The production was moved from Miami,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.560
Florida to Ixtapa, Mexico, where things only&nbsp;
got worse. Titanic, which was filming nearby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.240
ate up the local resources. Eventually, several&nbsp;
members of the cast and crew were fired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.020
and the water traps on a golf course&nbsp;
location were teeming with crocodiles.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.900
Still, the Mehrezes finished The&nbsp;
Good Life and released a promo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.201
reel that prominently featured Sylvester Stallone.

00:07:12.201 --> 00:07:14.891
"You wanna dance, you gotta&nbsp;
pay the band, you understand?"

00:07:14.891 --> 00:07:19.140
This displeased the action star and his&nbsp;
brother, who filed back-to-back lawsuits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:24.300
against the producers totaling $50 million. A&nbsp;
settlement was reached, but The Good Life never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:29.460
saw a theatrical release. Currently, a brief&nbsp;
17-second clip is available to view on YouTube.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.140
What happens when you bring together&nbsp;
two Oscar-nominated actors, a pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:38.040
acclaimed Saturday Night Live writers, and&nbsp;
a producer whose list of credits includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:43.260
everything from Fast Times at Ridgemont High to&nbsp;
Yellowstone? The answer is "disaster," that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:48.599
if you're talking about Arrive Alive, a 1990&nbsp;
comedy starring Willem Dafoe and Joan Cusack.

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:52.500
Produced by Art Linson and written by SNL&nbsp;
vets Michael O'Donoghue and Mitch Glazer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:56.280
the film was intended as a vehicle for Bill&nbsp;
Murray, who would have played a low-rent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:00.240
detective investigating a complex scheme&nbsp;
involving a Florida Native American tribe.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.135
"What are you looking for in a movie?"

00:08:02.135 --> 00:08:06.240
"Well, you know, you want manners. You really&nbsp;
do. You guys, there's gotta be manners involved."

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.740
According to Glazer, Murray passed in part&nbsp;
due to the film's violent content — one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.880
scene involved a killer whale biting off its&nbsp;
trainer's head — and Dafoe took over as lead.

00:08:15.067 --> 00:08:19.087
Three weeks after the start of production,&nbsp;
Paramount pulled the plug on Arrive Alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.087 --> 00:08:23.280
Sources differ on the exact reason,&nbsp;
but the studio allegedly wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.940
change the tone of O'Donoghue and Glazer's&nbsp;
script by adding more slapstick elements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.204 --> 00:08:31.344
Dafoe was reportedly uncomfortable with a&nbsp;
broader approach and left the production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.344 --> 00:08:35.640
leading to its cancellation. Regardless&nbsp;
of the reason, Arrive Alive exists today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.960
only in script form, although a YouTube video&nbsp;
claims to include three stills from the film.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:45.300
The thin line between passion project and&nbsp;
vanity film was underscored by Chinese real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.380
estate mogul Jon Jiang when he set to making&nbsp;
the fantasy feature Empires of the Deep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.644 --> 00:08:53.484
Conceived as the start of an epic franchise&nbsp;
like Star Wars or The Lord of the Rings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.484 --> 00:08:59.040
Jiang sunk the equivalent of $130 million into&nbsp;
the film, which concerned a battle between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.120
mermaid kingdoms and a demonic warlord.&nbsp;
Jiang used his deep pockets to initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.500
attach The Empire Strikes Back director Irvin&nbsp;
Kershner and Italian actress Monica Bellucci.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:12.432
Piles of money can buy talent but it can't make&nbsp;
them produce under impossible circumstances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.432 --> 00:09:17.160
Language barriers, dangerous conditions, and&nbsp;
constant, illogical script changes dictated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.300
by Jiang caused several directors and actors&nbsp;
to quit the production, which was ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:26.820
credited to three separate filmmakers. Worst of&nbsp;
all, a trailer was broadly mocked online for its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:31.440
subpar special effects and incomprehensible&nbsp;
plot. Empires failed to find distribution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.180
despite costly reshoots and a massive&nbsp;
crowdfunding campaign, and remains unreleased,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:40.440
though Jiang insisted as late as 2016&nbsp;
that the film was ready for distribution.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.720
Hoping to rebound from a string of costly&nbsp;
flops like the late 90s film Mercury Rising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:50.580
Bruce Willis signed on as both star and&nbsp;
producer of Broadway Brawler in 1997. The film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:54.960
intended as a sports-themed romantic comedy&nbsp;
a la Jerry Maguire, featured Maura Tierney as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:58.860
the love interest to Willis' washed-up ice&nbsp;
hockey champ, and Oscar winner Lee Grant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:04.500
as director. The $28 million project had all the&nbsp;
hallmarks of a hit until Willis pulled the plug.

00:10:04.734 --> 00:10:09.654
After 20 days of filming, Willis fired&nbsp;
Grant, cinematographer William A. Fraker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.654 --> 00:10:13.500
and several others over dissatisfaction&nbsp;
with their work. Dennis Dugan, who directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:18.480
numerous Adam Sandler comedies, was brought on&nbsp;
to replace Grant but lasted just a day before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:23.880
project was abandoned. Twenty-five years later,&nbsp;
Broadway Brawler remains unfinished and unseen.

00:10:24.516 --> 00:10:28.452
Though often cited as one of the greatest&nbsp;
filmmakers in motion picture history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.452 --> 00:10:32.400
Orson Welles frequently struggled to&nbsp;
complete his projects, and in many cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.780
failed to even get them off the ground. His&nbsp;
uncompromising artistic vision and mercurial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:41.220
personality led many financiers to withhold&nbsp;
or pull support for his work. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:46.740
Welles left many unfinished projects after his&nbsp;
death in 1985. Some, like The Other Side of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:51.120
Wind, were completed by collaborators, while&nbsp;
others, like The Deep, remain uncompleted.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:55.620
An adaptation of the novel Dead Calm by Charles&nbsp;
Williams — which was made into a film with Nicole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.620 --> 00:11:01.080
Kidman in 1989 — The Deep starred Welles, Jeanne&nbsp;
Moreau, and Laurence Harvey as ocean voyagers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:05.340
drawn together by murder and deception. Welles&nbsp;
directed the film on a sailboat off the coast of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:11.220
Yugoslavia between 1966 and 1969. But financial&nbsp;
and technical problems prevented Welles from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.720
completing production, and the death of Harvey&nbsp;
in 1973, along with the loss of the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:20.640
negative, torpedoed any chance of salvaging&nbsp;
the project. Other than clips included in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.220
1995 documentary Orson Welles: One-Man Band, The&nbsp;
Deep has gone unseen for more than five decades.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:32.220
In the mid-1980s, late night television viewers&nbsp;
in certain markets had their senses assaulted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:36.600
a commercial for Blood Circus, an ultra-low-budget&nbsp;
feature that appeared to mix professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:42.240
wrestling, aliens, bargain basement gore, and a&nbsp;
rock singer shrieking about gold jewelry. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:47.280
was a vanity project from Santo Victor Rigatuso,&nbsp;
a notorious figure in the as-seen-on-TV market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:51.734
who created Blood Circus as a promotional&nbsp;
vehicle for his Santo Gold jewelry line.

00:11:51.734 --> 00:11:55.800
"The Santo Gold heavyweight championship&nbsp;
wrestling belt is awarded to the winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:00.720
tag team in the new science fiction space&nbsp;
wrassling movie comedy Blood Circus.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:04.800
Santo Gold was the subject of numerous&nbsp;
federal cease-and-desist orders for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:08.700
outlandish claims made about the jewelry,&nbsp;
which was essentially gold-plated metal.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:13.620
Rigatuso reportedly spent a fortune on Blood&nbsp;
Circus, but failed to land a distributor for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:18.840
the film. Reviews that called the picture&nbsp;
incomprehensible probably didn't help. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:22.560
Blood Circus played for a week at several&nbsp;
theaters in Baltimore before disappearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:27.480
although clips are available on YouTube.&nbsp;
Rigatuso, who later spent time in prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:31.860
for mail fraud charges, continues to promote&nbsp;
Blood Circus through his ramshackle website.

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:37.140
Few animated features have had a streak of bad&nbsp;
luck like DreamWorks' Me and My Shadow. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:42.060
film began its convoluted existence in 2010 as a&nbsp;
comedic fantasy tale about a human who discovers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:46.680
that his shadow has a life of its own. Conceived&nbsp;
as a mix of traditional hand-drawn animation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:51.300
CGI, Me and My Shadow landed Josh Gad and Bill&nbsp;
Hader to voice its human and shadow heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:55.680
with Kate Hudson as Gad's love interest.&nbsp;
Initially slated for a 2013 release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.740
Shadow was pushed to 2014 to&nbsp;
make room for the release of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:02.340
Mr. Peabody and Sherman. When that&nbsp;
film flopped, the troubles began.

00:13:02.808 --> 00:13:05.855
"I don't want this one, Mommy. He's sarcastic."

00:13:05.855 --> 00:13:10.200
DreamWorks Animation underwent a massive&nbsp;
restructuring that saw the loss of 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.920
jobs and the cancellation of several&nbsp;
animated projects, including Shadow.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.600
But the story didn't end there: Edgar&nbsp;
Wright revived the project in 2015 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:22.080
wrote three drafts of a new script&nbsp;
before Comcast bought Dreamworks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:26.040
ordered another round of restructuring.&nbsp;
In a cinematic case of collateral damage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:30.840
Shadow was canceled for the second and&nbsp;
final time. Approximately 12 minutes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.780
test animation and concept galleries&nbsp;
are all that remain of the movie.

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:38.700
Though its title suggests a sequel&nbsp;
to 1999's 10 Things I Hate About You,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:44.340
the 2012 romantic dramedy 10 Things I Hate About&nbsp;
Life was, in fact, a stand-alone film made by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:48.900
some of the same creatives responsible for the&nbsp;
1999 movie, including director Gil Junger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:53.280
producer Andrew Lazar. Evan Rachel Wood and&nbsp;
Thomas McDonnell were cast as the despondent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:57.240
lead characters who find a reason to live in&nbsp;
each other, and funding came in part from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:01.800
pair of independent companies, the U.K.-based&nbsp;
Intandem Films and Mad Chance Productions.

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:06.060
Filming began in late 2012 but was&nbsp;
halted in early 2013 when Intandem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:09.900
chief Gary Smith stepped down as the&nbsp;
company faced financial losses. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:14.700
in conversations with industry press, Smith's&nbsp;
replacement, Robert Mitchell, cited Evan Rachel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:19.800
Wood's pregnancy as the reason for the shutdown&nbsp;
and claimed production would resume in 2013.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.840
By 2014, the only active component of 10 Things&nbsp;
I Hate About Life was a lawsuit filed against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:29.700
Wood by Intandem for breach of contract after she&nbsp;
left the film before completion. Wood fired back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:33.960
saying that Intandem had failed to pay her. The&nbsp;
standoff resulted in the demise of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:38.640
which exists today only as a trailer and&nbsp;
production photos. A 30-minute segment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:42.720
screened for potential investors at the&nbsp;
American Film Market in 2013, appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:47.699
have disappeared without a trace, though the&nbsp;
original screenplay surfaced online in 2022.

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:52.860
An unlikely partnership between Jamie Foxx&nbsp;
and Ken Jeong resulted in not one but two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:57.840
unreleased feature films. Oscar winner Foxx and&nbsp;
Afterparty 2 star Jeong announced an informal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:01.560
collaboration in 2012 that would involve each&nbsp;
appearing in a film that the other wrote:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:05.700
Foxx would star in Jeong's comedy After&nbsp;
Prom, and Jeong would co-star in Foxx's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:09.240
All-Star Weekend. Jeong's film appears&nbsp;
to have vanished — there's no information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:15.000
about it after 2013 – while All-Star Weekend was&nbsp;
completed in 2016 and slated for a 2018 release.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:20.340
But the comedy, which stars Foxx and Jeremy&nbsp;
Piven as NBA obsessives, was shelved for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:24.480
indefinite future, despite having A-listers&nbsp;
like Robert Downey, Jr., Eva Longoria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:29.520
and Benicio Del Toro in the cast. It's possible&nbsp;
that some of the film's humor contributed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:34.020
its status. Coverage of the film noted that&nbsp;
Downey played, quote, "a Mexican" in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:38.760
while Foxx, who played multiple characters,&nbsp;
appeared as a, quote, "white, racist cop."

00:15:38.935 --> 00:15:41.935
When asked about Weekend&nbsp;
by Cinemablend, Foxx said,

00:15:41.935 --> 00:15:46.211
"We're trying to break open those sensitive&nbsp;
corners where people go back to laughing again."

00:15:46.211 --> 00:15:49.020
What Foxx meant by "sensitive corners" is unclear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:51.420
but All-Star Weekend is in&nbsp;
limbo for the time being.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:56.460
Randy Quaid was still a beloved,&nbsp;
Oscar-nominated character actor in 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.580
when he teamed up with Michael Caine and&nbsp;
Catherine McCormick to star in The Debtors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:03.900
a comedy about individuals with addiction&nbsp;
issues. The film had only a handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:07.620
screenings, which generated such a negative&nbsp;
response that the project was shelved.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:12.180
The decision to pull the plug on The Debtors was&nbsp;
reportedly rooted in a conflict between Evi Quaid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:16.740
and software billionaire Charles Simonyi, who&nbsp;
invested more than $12 million into the project.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:20.640
"Don Bigbucks. Chance wanted&nbsp;
me to sit in for him."

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:23.700
Simonyi was apparently displeased&nbsp;
with some of the film's content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:28.620
including a fairly graphic sequence at a concert&nbsp;
featuring the German industrial band Rammstein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:32.220
and won an injunction against the Quaids&nbsp;
that prevented them from releasing the film.

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:35.940
The Quaids, who'd invested more than $3&nbsp;
million of their own money in The Debtors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:40.320
were forced to declare bankruptcy, marking the&nbsp;
beginning of years of personal and professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:44.700
downfalls. Though The Debtors has gone unseen&nbsp;
since the injunction, the Quaids attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:49.260
to make the film available via the streaming&nbsp;
platform DaCast. More recently, it was listed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:53.520
on the Indian streaming service Binged, which&nbsp;
noted that the film is "not streaming in India."

